course: reflection
lesson: 8
title: Scary things in the world: news, strangers, war and what-ifs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some fears come from things that happened to us, and some from things we hear about: a drill, a news report, a video, half a sentence from grown-ups in the kitchen; the mind fills the gaps with the worst version.
explain: Max heard his parents talking about money and "losing the house", and for two weeks he had tummy aches and couldn't concentrate, lying awake thinking, "Where will we live? Will we be homeless?"
explain: When he finally told Mum what he'd heard, she explained they had a plan and he would be safe, and the tummy aches stopped; children often hear a bit of a grown-up worry and imagine the whole disaster.
explain: The useful habit is to ask "Is this real, how likely is it, and what would I do?": a drill is practice, a scary YouTube "end of the world" is clickbait, but a man in a car offering a lift is a real danger, and running home and telling was exactly right.
explain: Some children carry very heavy memories, like war, for a long time; that is not weakness, and special help, such as a counsellor and a kind classmate, can make it lighter.
fact: Lockdown drills and fire drills are practice so everyone knows what to do; schools practise because it is sensible, not because something bad is coming, and real emergencies are very rare.
fact: Never get into a car with a stranger; running away and telling a grown-up straight away is right, and "trust your gut" means trust the feeling that something is wrong.
fact: A refugee is someone who had to leave their country to be safe, for example because of war.
fact: Trauma is a very deep hurt from something terrifying; it can last a long time, it is not weakness, and help from a counsellor can make it lighter.
fact: Ice on ponds can look strong but break; never walk on frozen ponds or lakes.
fact: Children often hear bits of grown-up worries and imagine the worst; asking a parent usually helps, and money problems are a grown-up job to sort out.
fact: Big waves are scary, but boats are built to handle rough ("choppy") water; seasickness makes fear feel worse.
fact: Seeing violence is upsetting even if you were not involved; the brain replays it to try to understand, and talking about it helps it fade.
fact: Many online videos say scary things that are not true to get views; clickbait is a scary or exciting title made to get clicks, and a conspiracy theory is a false story about a secret plan.
fact: Videos have claimed "the world will end" many times for years, and it has not happened; checking facts with a trusted adult helps.
fact: Dentists sometimes cause a little pain to stop much worse pain later; telling the dentist you're scared, and agreeing a stop signal, helps.
fact: In a scary emergency many people freeze for a moment; shouting for a grown-up and learning basic first aid help children feel ready.
exercise: After a lockdown drill, older kids told Noah scary stories. Why do schools do drills? | It is practice so everyone knows what to do; real ones are rare | Because the teachers have been told that something bad is coming very soon | To scare children into behaving well in class | Because every school has a real emergency each month
exercise: A man in a car followed Chloe and asked if she wanted a lift. She ran home. Was that right? | Yes; never get in a car with a stranger, and tell a grown-up | No; she should have been polite to him and just accepted the kind offer of a lift | No; she should have asked him where he was going first | Yes, but she shouldn't tell anyone in case it's a fuss
exercise: Chloe's mum called the police. How did the police respond? | They thanked Chloe for being brave and reporting it | They told her off for wasting their time | They said running away was rude to the man | They said children should never tell the police anything
exercise: Kofi's family fled a war, and months later he still has nightmares. What is true? | Carrying fear after war is not weakness, and a counsellor can help | He should be over it by now, because he's safe | Asking him lots of questions about the war will help | Nightmares mean he is making the memories up
exercise: What helped Kofi? | A counsellor in his language, drawing and a kind classmate | Classmates asking him about the war every day so he could talk it all out | Being told to forget his home country | Watching news about the war to stay informed
exercise: Sophie fell through ice on a pond up to her knees. What's the safety lesson? | Never walk on frozen ponds; ice can look strong but break | Ice is always safe to walk on if it looks white and thick enough | Only walk on ice when nobody can see you | Ice breaks only for heavy grown-ups, not children
exercise: Max overheard "losing the house" and got tummy aches for two weeks. What helped? | Telling Mum what he heard, so she could explain the plan | Worrying in secret about it so that he wouldn't upset anyone at home | Saving his pocket money to buy a new house | Pretending he never heard anything at all
exercise: After a storm on a boat trip, Ella hates boats. What is true? | Boats are built for big waves; seasickness made it feel worse | Boats nearly always tip over in a storm | She should go out on a big boat in a storm straight away to cure it | Being seasick means something is wrong with her
exercise: Theo saw a fight outside the chippy and keeps seeing the blood at night. Why? | The brain replays a shock to try to understand it | He must have secretly started the fight himself somehow | It means he will be in a real fight himself very soon | Seeing a fight once makes you a violent person forever
exercise: What helped Theo? | Telling Dad, who explained the police dealt with it | Keeping it all inside so nobody would worry | Watching fight videos online to get used to seeing blood | Going back to the chippy to look for the men
exercise: Amelia's friend says the world will end in a year because of a YouTube video. What is true? | Such videos are often clickbait; it's been "ending" for years | If it's on YouTube with lots of views, scientists must agree with it | She should watch more videos to be sure | Nobody can ever check whether a video is true
exercise: What is clickbait? | A scary or exciting title made to get clicks | A safe website that has been checked by teachers | A news report that has been proven to be true | A game where you have to click as fast as you can
exercise: Lucas had a painful dentist visit and says he'll let his teeth fall out. What helped? | A kind dentist explaining each step and a signal to stop | Hiding his fear and never going back | Brushing much harder every day so he never needs to see a dentist | Pulling out his wobbly teeth himself
exercise: Lily's friend fell off a wall and was knocked out for a minute. Lily froze. What is true? | Freezing is normal; shouting for an adult and first aid help | She should blame herself for not being a doctor | Freezing like that means she doesn't really care about her friend at all | Children should never call for help in emergencies
test: A Year 6 says, "lockdown drill means a bad man's definitely coming, that's why." What's the truth? | Drills are practice; real emergencies are very rare | He's right; schools only practise when they already know about a danger | Drills mean the school is unsafe every day | He's right, and Noah should stay home from school
test: Chloe's friend says, "if a man offers a lift and he seems nice, it's fine, innit?" What's right? | No; never get in a stranger's car, even a nice one's | Yes; people who look nice and friendly are always safe | Yes, as long as it is raining hard and you're really tired | Yes, if he already knows your name and which school you go to
test: What would happen if Max kept hearing worrying bits through the wall and never asked? | He'd probably keep imagining the worst on his own | He'd work out the full story by himself, bit by bit | His parents' money problems would simply disappear by themselves | His tummy aches would go away faster all on their own
test: Ella's Canadian uncle says, "eh, it was a bit choppy, that's all." ("Choppy" means rough water.) What helped Ella in the end? | Talking about it and later a small calm boat trip | Forcing herself onto a huge boat in a storm to get over it | Never going near the water again for the rest of her life | Watching lots of shipwreck films every night before bed
test: Theo's mate goes, "ngl I'd be well shook too." ("Shook" means shocked.) Is it normal to be shocked by a fight you only watched? | Yes; seeing violence upsets people even if they weren't involved | No; only people in the fight can feel shocked | No; watching a fight is always exciting, not upsetting | Yes, but only if you knew the men fighting
test: In a cartoon, a boy jumps up and down on a frozen pond and is totally fine. What's the safety truth? | Ice can look strong but break, so never walk on frozen ponds | Ice is always safe if you jump fast enough | Ponds only crack like that in films and cartoons, never in real life | Ice holds anyone under the age of twelve
test: Amelia's mum from Singapore says, "Aiyah, the world ending one ah? Can check or not?" ("Can check or not" means can we check it.) What did Mum do that helped? | Showed her how to check facts and a science video about it | Agreed the world will end and booked a holiday | Told her to stop asking and go to bed | Watched more end-of-world videos with her
test: Kofi's classmate keeps asking, "was there bombs? did you see dead people?" What's the kinder thing to do? | Not push him with war questions; just be a kind classmate | Keep asking so he gets it all out | Tell the whole class his story for him | Avoid him so you don't say the wrong thing
test: Lucas's gran says, "back in my day the dentist didn't numb nothing." ("Numbing" is medicine so it doesn't hurt.) What helps Lucas now? | Telling the dentist he's scared and agreeing a stop signal | Never going to the dentist again | Pretending to be brave and saying nothing | Asking for no numbing to toughen up
test: Lily thinks, "I froze. I'm useless in an emergency." What's a fairer thought? | Many people freeze; learning first aid will help me | Freezing proves I'll never be able to help anyone ever | Only grown-ups are allowed to help out in any kind of emergency | I should have picked my friend up and carried her home myself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a school lockdown drill? | Rehearsal of procedures so that all persons know what to do | Advance notification of a danger known to be imminent | A disciplinary measure intended to frighten pupils | A response to emergencies which occur in schools each month
exam: It is given that an unknown motorist has offered a child conveyance in his vehicle. Determine the course of action consistent with established safety guidance. | Refusal, departure from the vicinity, and prompt report to a trusted adult | Acceptance of the offer where the motorist appears courteous | Acceptance of the offer in conditions of inclement weather | Concealment of the incident in order to avoid inconvenience
exam: Identify the principal reason for which children frequently suffer anxiety upon overhearing parental conversations concerning finances. | Partial information is supplemented by the child's imagining of the worst | Children are customarily responsible for the household finances | Parental conversations concerning money are invariably accurate predictions | Children are incapable of any comprehension of financial matters
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a video which predicts the end of the world within one year. | Such claims have recurred for many years without fulfilment and warrant verification | Such claims are reliable where the video has received numerous views | Such claims have been confirmed by the scientific community | Such claims cannot under any circumstances be examined
